# =============================================================================
# BUILD CONTEXT EXCLUSIONS FOR `services/core-api/Dockerfile`.
#
# WHY A SECOND IGNORE FILE INSTEAD OF EDITING THE ROOT `.dockerignore`.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# BuildKit looks for `<dockerfile-path>.dockerignore` FIRST and, when it finds
# one, uses it INSTEAD of the root `.dockerignore` — not in addition to it. So
# this file is the complete rule set for this one build and the Edge's rules
# are untouched.
#
# That is the point. The root `.dockerignore` is deny-by-default and admits
# exactly `packages/contracts` and `services/edge-runtime`; a central build
# needs `services/core-api` admitted instead. Editing the root file to admit
# both would mean every future Edge image carried core-api — the whole Prisma
# toolchain, every module, every migration — into the build context of a box in
# a customer's wiring closet, in order to build a service that does not use any
# of it. Two builds with genuinely different needs get two rule sets.
#
# Same discipline as the root file, and for the same reason: EXCLUDE
# EVERYTHING, admit by name what the Dockerfile needs, then re-exclude the
# dangerous things that live inside the admitted directories. Later patterns
# win. A file added to this repository can never widen this image by accident —
# it can only be admitted by an edit here, which is a reviewable diff.
# =============================================================================

# ---- 1. Nothing is in the context unless named below. -----------------------
*

# ---- 2. Workspace metadata pnpm needs for a frozen, filtered install. -------
!package.json
!pnpm-workspace.yaml
!pnpm-lock.yaml
!tsconfig.base.json

# ---- 3. The only two workspace members central is built from. ---------------
# NOT apps/, NOT services/edge-runtime, NOT tests/, NOT docs/, NOT .github/,
# NOT scripts/, NOT infrastructure/ — none of which are excluded by a rule
# naming them, but by never having been admitted. `.git` is in that same set:
# the whole history, every branch, and every secret ever committed and later
# removed is the single largest thing a careless container build leaks. It is
# not here.
!packages/contracts
!services/core-api

# ---- 4. Re-exclusions INSIDE the two admitted directories. ------------------

# Host-built artefacts. `node_modules` from a Windows/macOS dev machine carries
# platform-specific binaries — Prisma's query engine above all — that would
# silently poison a linux/amd64 image; `dist` from a dev machine is code nobody
# can attribute to a commit. Both are rebuilt inside the build stage from the
# lockfile, which is the point.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/build
**/coverage
**/.turbo
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Package-manager caches. A pnpm/npm store inside a layer is tens of megabytes
# of tarballs the runtime cannot use and an attacker can read for an exact
# dependency inventory of the box.
**/.pnpm-store
**/.npm
**/node_modules/.cache

# TEST TOOLING. Specs never run in the image. `*.test-support.ts` is called out
# separately because it is NOT matched by tsconfig's `**/*.spec.ts` exclusion
# and would therefore be COMPILED into `dist` — and in this service those files
# are not inert: `modules/edge-trusted-time/edge-trusted-time.test-support.ts`
# generates P-256 anchor signing keypairs. Nothing outside a spec imports any
# of them, so their absence cannot break the build.
**/*.spec.ts
**/*.test.ts
**/*.test-support.ts
**/test-integration-support.ts
**/vitest.config.*
**/__tests__
**/__fixtures__
services/core-api/test

# SECRETS. Belt and braces: nothing under the admitted directories should ever
# be a key, and if one appears there anyway it does not reach the daemon. The
# central trusted-time signing key arrives by MOUNT at runtime — see the
# Dockerfile's `/run/secrets` note — and has no business in a layer.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p8
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/id_rsa*
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/secrets
**/*.secret

# Editor / OS noise that would otherwise bust the layer cache on every commit.
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
**/*.swp
**/.idea
**/.vscode

# The Dockerfile and this file. Both are read by the frontend before the
# context is assembled, so neither is ever needed inside it.
services/core-api/Dockerfile
services/core-api/Dockerfile.dockerignore
